# Read by Cloudflare at deploy time and not served. It applies to static
# files, including the two pages the Worker reads through its ASSETS binding
# (/ and /privacy.html get the /* rule and their Content-Type rule). Responses the Worker builds itself
# (/mcp, /a2a, /api/v1, the markdown variants) set their own headers in code.
#
# When several rules match one URL, their values for the same header are joined
# with a comma. So a header set under /* must not be set again further down,
# and no two rules below may match the same URL.

# What GitHub Pages sent on every file, plus nosniff and the browser security
# headers. Every file gets them, so a page served from any path has them.
#   Referrer-Policy     only the origin goes to other sites, never the path
#   X-Frame-Options     no site may frame these pages (clickjacking)
#   Permissions-Policy  the pages use none of these features; Turnstile needs
#                       none of them either
#   COOP                a window this site opens, or that opens it, gets no
#                       handle to it. Nothing here uses window.opener.
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is deliberately not set: require-corp would
# block Google Analytics and the Turnstile iframe, which send no CORP header.
#
# Content-Security-Policy, one line because _headers has no continuation:
#   script-src   this site; the two inline Google scripts in <head>, by hash
#                (identical in index.html, privacy.html and 404.html;
#                tests/csp.test.ts fails if one changes and its hash here does
#                not); gtag.js and gtm.js; and Turnstile's api.js. The GTM
#                container has no tags, so it injects nothing else.
#   style-src    this site, plus the style attribute on the <noscript> GTM
#                iframe, by hash
#   img-src,     where Google Analytics sends hits, including the Google
#   connect-src  signals endpoints, per Google's CSP guide for GA4
#   frame-src    the Turnstile widget, and the <noscript> GTM iframe
#   frame-ancestors 'none' is the modern form of X-Frame-Options: DENY.
/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-9DQ65YRPbIVBCGUpoJsg5I6gFiU6zARAGqyEOk4j2ZA=' 'sha256-M+tdapiSSVJwzAkTR7nHcm6tIhiKAOB/MtpHtqGMkts=' https://www.googletagmanager.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-hashes' 'sha256-kELgoK46JmGjLd8UHfzN0qJToDgIB+yMtRHG8PtGL7s='; img-src 'self' data: https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.googletagmanager.com https://*.g.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com https://*.googletagmanager.com https://*.g.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# The pages. In production the asset router sends a bare "text/html" (and
# "text/css", "text/javascript"): no charset, so the browser falls back to the
# <meta charset> in each file. Stated here so the header carries it too.
# "/" and "/privacy" are what the Worker reads through its binding for "/" and
# "/privacy.html", so these rules reach both pages. A path with no file gets
# 404.html under its own path, which no exact rule can name; that page has its
# <meta charset> in its first line of <head>.
/
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

/privacy
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

/404
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

# Without these every file is revalidated on every view. URLs are not
# versioned, so code gets an hour and images a month. Both files contain
# non-ASCII characters, so the charset is stated as well.
/static/css/*
  Content-Type: text/css; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/static/js/*
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/static/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# Markdown. Cloudflare infers the type without a charset; stated here because
# the files contain non-ASCII punctuation.
/index.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/privacy.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/auth.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/developers.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/.well-known/agent-skills/contact-burrows-applications/SKILL.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# Documents written by workers/agent-readiness/tools/write-documents.ts.
# The first four have no file extension, so no type is inferred for them: the
# Content-Type lines here are what make them usable.
/.well-known/api-catalog
  Content-Type: application/linkset+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
  Link: </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog"

/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/mcp/server-card
  Content-Type: application/mcp-server-card+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET
  Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, If-None-Match
  Access-Control-Expose-Headers: ETag

/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET
  Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, If-None-Match
  Access-Control-Expose-Headers: ETag

/.well-known/agent-card.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET
  Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, If-None-Match
  Access-Control-Expose-Headers: ETag
